; `if` and `cond` are the two lazy special forms with a value, and both
; have a defined answer for "no branch matched" that nothing else in the
; corpus states.
;
; Form 2 is a two-arg `if` with a false test: the value is nil, not an
; error and not the untaken branch. Form 7 is the same story for `cond`
; with every test false. So both forms are total, and a document that
; leans on them gets a nil it has to handle rather than a missing result.
;
; Forms 3-5 pin truthiness, which `Value.isTruthy` defines as "everything
; except `false` and `nil`". So (if 0 1 2) is 1 and (if "" 1 2) is 1 —
; zero, the empty string and the empty vector are all truthy, and a C or
; JavaScript reflex about any of them is wrong here. Form 6 is the only
; non-boolean that is falsy.
(if true 1 2)
(if false 1 2)
(if false 1)
(if 0 1 2)
(if "" 1 2)
(if [] 1 2)
(if nil 1 2)
(cond false 1 true 2)
(cond false 1)
